id: decisions/017-vocabulary-closure
title: "A compared field is closed, and grows only by registry"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'vocabulary-closure'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: vocabulary-closure
axis_gloss: "whether a term set is fixed and enumerable, or may grow freely as records need new words"
poles:
  a: open-vocabulary
  b: closed-vocabulary
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 14.4
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(12) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Which term sets in a .yawn are closed lists — status: and epistemic_status now
  are, under tests — and is kind: one of them, or may a record coin its own kind
  whenever it has a new purpose?

why_it_matters: >
  kind: is the field a bot would use to ask 'show me every receipt' or 'every
  contract', and today twenty-six records answer with thirteen words and twelve
  core files with eleven, while the RFC index carries a status its own lifecycle
  diagram does not list and the CHANGELOG has a section no convention names.
  Once answered, every child Yawn's kinds are enumerable, so an index, a ranker
  or a steward bot can operate over them without reading prose, and a new kind
  is a recorded act instead of a drift.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    5 judgments sit at open-vocabulary (0 authored) and 7 at closed-vocabulary (3
    authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave wants the registry in templates/node.yawn beside record_shapes or
    in its own core/ record; and which of the near-duplicate receipt kinds are the
    same kind under different names.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Vocabulary is open by default. Outside the fields a test already closes
      (status, epistemic_status, authored_by, record_shape), a record may coin its
      own kind, an RFC its own status string, a CHANGELOG its own section. Closure
      is the exception and applies only where a test names the enum.
    cost: "Nothing to change; write in templates/node.yawn that kind: is free text."
    risk: "The same drift that produced 50+ epistemic values and 38 status values recurs in kind:, and the rationale the repository already recorded — a vocabulary is only computable while it is closed — is contradicted for the one field an index most needs."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce openness; the record would be a line in templates/node.yawn."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0271"
      - "J0630"
      - "J0911"
      - "J0936"
      - "J0952"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Every field a ranker, index or bot compares across records draws from a
      closed list held by a test with a shrink-only grandfather ledger, on the
      pattern of tests/epistemic-status-vocabulary.test.mjs: status (done),
      epistemic_status (done), authored_by (done), record_shape (done), kind:
      (closed to a fixed set), RFC status (the eight stages rfcs/README.md already
      names), CHANGELOG sections (Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed,
      Security). No new value without a test change.
    cost: "A kind test with a by-file ledger seeded from roughly forty distinct kinds; a rewrite of RFC 0002's status line; a Removed section in CHANGELOG.md for the fifty retired files; every new record purpose needs a test edit."
    risk: "A frozen kind enum is wrong for a growing protocol — a genuinely new record purpose is a legitimate new kind — so writers reuse the nearest wrong kind to avoid touching a test."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/kind-vocabulary.test.mjs with a fixed KINDS set and by-file ledger; a test that every status under rfcs/README.md Current RFCs is one of its Lifecycle stages; a test that CHANGELOG.md section headings are within the Keep a Changelog set."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0421"
      - "J0681"
      - "J0886"
      - "J0907"
      - "J1088"
      - "J1177"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: A field is closed once it is compared across records; free text stays
      free where nothing compares it (title, purpose, notes). kind: joins status,
      epistemic_status, authored_by and record_shape as a closed field, but closed
      by registry, not by frozen enum: every kind is listed with a one-line
      definition in a registry record (templates/node.yawn kinds:, or
      core/vocabulary.yawn), and a new kind may be added only by adding it to the
      registry in the same change. So the vocabulary grows by record, never by
      drift. RFC statuses and CHANGELOG sections follow the lists their own index
      documents already state.
    cost: "A registry seeded with today's kinds, each needing a definition (which will expose near-duplicates such as proof-receipt, bounded-action-proof-receipt, owner-decision-receipt); one test; one RFC status line; one CHANGELOG section."
    risk: "The registry is edited as casually as the records were, and closure is nominal; the definitions are the only thing that makes adding a kind a decision."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "tests/kind-vocabulary.test.mjs: every kind: in a tracked .yawn appears in the registry, and every registry entry has a non-empty definition; no grandfather ledger needed because the registry is seeded from today's values; plus the RFC and CHANGELOG assertions from B."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0907"
      - "J1177"
      - "J0681"
      - "J0886"
      - "J0271"
      - "J0630"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.8
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The passing suite has now closed a vocabulary four times — epistemic_status,
    status, authored_by, record_shape — each with the same authored rationale,
    stated in the commit and in the test comment: free text makes records
    uncomputable, no ranker or ledger can operate over an open vocabulary (J0907,
    J1177). Side A is entirely REVEALED drift, and one of its five judgments
    (J0911, free-text status) has already been removed by
    tests/status-vocabulary.test.mjs since the inventory ran. J0681 (authored)
    shows Dave's instinct is to split and define nine relational terms rather than
    leave one loose word. So the authored side is closure. C over B because kind:
    genuinely grows — a new record purpose is a legitimate new kind — so the
    honest mechanism is a registry that must be edited on purpose with a
    definition, not a frozen enum that writers route around. The definitions will
    do the real work: the receipt kinds alone have four near-synonyms today.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "A registry (templates/node.yawn kinds: or core/vocabulary.yawn) lists every kind: in use with a one-line definition."
    - "A test in tests/ rejects any kind: value not in the registry and any registry entry without a definition, and passes."
    - "rfcs/README.md Current RFCs carries only statuses its own Lifecycle names; CHANGELOG.md records the fifty retired files under Removed."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A record with a kind: never seen before lands in the tree and npm test stays
    green; or the registry gains entries with empty definitions.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0271"
  - "J0630"
  - "J0911"
  - "J0936"
  - "J0952"
  - "J0421"
  - "J0681"
  - "J0886"
  - "J0907"
  - "J1088"
  - "J1177"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
